XPost Internals: the module map
==============================

What this file is for. It is the map: which module holds what, how the
modules layer, and the shape of the things at the bottom that nothing
else describes -- the memory file, its table, the special entities, the
free list, the stacks and the save stack.

doc/NEWINTERNALS is the other half and the one to read next: the object
itself, the tunable parameters, the operator dispatch, the devices, the
recorded page and the band loop. doc/ROOTS is the collector's root set,
bank by bank. Where a subject appears in both files, this one places it
and NEWINTERNALS explains it.


Running out of the source tree
------------------------------

Xpost finds the PostScript files it boots from by looking for init.ps,
in this order (xpost_interpreter.c:xpost_interpreter_data_dir):

    $XPOST_DATA_DIR
    the directory worked out from the shared library's own location
    the configured PACKAGE_DATA_DIR
    data, ../data, ../../data, relative to the working directory

The second of those is what makes a build tree self-sufficient. It is
computed in xpost_main.c from where the library was loaded: an installed
one looks beside itself for ../share/xpost, and where that is not there
-- an uninstalled build -- it tries ../../../data instead, which is the
source tree's own data directory from either build/src/lib or
src/lib/.libs. So an interpreter built and not installed finds the
tree's PostScript files from any working directory, and a change to one
of them takes effect without installing anything. A shell alias is
enough to keep one to hand:

    alias xpost="$PWD/build/src/bin/xpost"

The same fallback is a trap for anything that reads the tree
programmatically. A guard handed the wrong source root does not fail --
it finds a working tree by one of those routes and reports a perfectly
true result about a tree the caller did not mean. tests/guard-paths.sh
exists for that reason; see doc/CONTRIBUTING.md.


